A range loop counts through numbers.

`to` stops before the end value:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 0 to 5 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This produces `0`, `1`, `2`, `3` and `4`.

Use `to &` when the end value should be included:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 0 to & 5 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This also produces `5`.

### Start from zero

You can leave out the first `0`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop to 5 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

### Change the step

Use `by` to set a signed step:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 0 to 10 by 2 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This produces `0`, `2`, `4`, `6` and `8`.

### Count downward

Use a negative step:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 5 to 0 by -1 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

This produces `5`, `4`, `3`, `2` and `1`.

The step's sign chooses direction. The bounds never do, so `loop 5 to 0` visits
nothing and `loop 5 to & 0 by -1` includes `0`. You do not need a `reverse`
keyword.

Any range using `F16`, `F32` or `F64` needs an explicit float step:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    loop 0.0 to 1.0 by 0.1 |amount|:
        io.line([: [amount]])
    ;
]

The value binding uses the selected range type. The optional second binding
counts visited iterations from zero and always has type `I32`, even when the
range counter uses another exact type.

Fixed-width bounds follow the exact numeric domain rules. Steps are signed whole
numbers from `I8` through `I64`. An omitted step is one, and unsigned, `Byte`,
`Dec` or float steps do not apply to an integer range. A literal zero step is
rejected during compilation. Any other zero step fails before the first iteration.
Counter updates end at the last valid endpoint rather than
overflowing. Ranges run on HTML-JS. HTML-Wasm lowers integer, `F32` and `F64`
counters, and rejects `F16` counters or recoverable range failure with a
capability diagnostic. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
